      "text": "To find the value of $v$ for which $\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}$ is a root of the quadratic expression $5x^2 + 21x + v$, we substitute $x = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}$ into the quadratic equation and solve for $v$.\n\nFirst, let's calculate $\\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}$ and $\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}$:\n$$\n\\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10} = 2.1 + 0.1\\sqrt{301},\n$$\n$$\n\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} = -2.1 - 0.1\\sqrt{301}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values of $x$ into the quadratic expression, we get:\n$$\n5\\left(-2.1-0.1\\sqrt{301}\\right)^2 + 21\\left(-2.1-0.1\\sqrt{301}\\right) + v.\n$$\nExpanding it, we get:\n$$\n5\\left(2.1+\\sqrt{301}\\right)\\left(2.1-\\sqrt{301}\\right) + 21\\left(-2.1-0.1\\sqrt{301}\\right) + v.\n$$\nNow, using $(a+b)(a-b)=a^2-b^2$:\n$$\nv = 5\\left(4.41-301\\right) + 21\\left(-4.2\\right) + v.\n$$\nSimplifying further:\n$$\nv = -1601 - 88.2 + v,\n$$\n$$\nv = -1696.2.\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $v$ is $\\boxed{-1696.2}$.",
